            <ArticleTitle>Glossopharyngeal neuralgia following foreign body impaction in the neck.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>70-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Glossopharyngeal neuralgia is rare, typically idiopathic and treated with carbamazepine. Surgery to decompress or transect the glossopharyngeal nerve root may be performed if conservative management fails. We present a case following trauma to the neck with foreign body impaction. To our knowledge this is the first case of glossopharyngeal neuralgia due to neck trauma.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
